# Machine handoff

Moves a session from one machine to another. The agent surveys and writes; the
owner runs. The agent never commits, never pushes, never stashes — it lists
every repository that would be updated and emits one script for the owner to
read before running.

## Phase 0 — Locate the checkouts

Drive this off the live filesystem, never off a remembered registry: enumerate
every git directory under the connector root (or under the repository root named
in `surfcontext.json` `projects[]`). A repository that was added since the last
handoff must show up without anyone editing this playbook.

## Phase 1 — Survey, read-only

For each checkout: current branch; whether it tracks a remote and whether it is
ahead, behind or diverged; `git status --short`; any stashes; and whether the
branch exists on the remote. Read-only commands only. Record the results; do not
act on them.

## Phase 2 — List what would change

Present a table: repository, branch, files changed, ahead/behind counts, and
whether a push would create a new remote branch. The owner decides from this
table; nothing is run yet.

## Phase 3 — Write the handoff document

Include: how to resume (the first three commands on the receiving machine); the
state of every repository at handoff; the work in progress and what is blocking
it; known issues; and — separately and prominently — any **stashes**, because a
stash does not travel with a push and will be left behind.

Give it `answers:` frontmatter, and put it where the receiving session will look:
the workspace when `home` is `surfspace`, otherwise the checkpoints directory.

## Phase 4 — Write the script (the agent writes; the OWNER runs)

One script, readable top to bottom, that for each repository with work: shows the
diff summary, stages the named paths explicitly rather than everything, commits
with a message the owner can edit, and pushes. It must be safe to read and
obvious to abort. Never add an AI co-author trailer or any AI attribution to a
commit message it writes.

Print the script's path and the exact command to run it. Do not run it.

## Phase 5 — Receiving machine

Step 1 is physical: mount the drive holding the checkouts and confirm it is
there. Step 2: confirm the keys named by `workspace.key_env` and any other
required environment variables are present in the login environment — not in a
shell that happens to be open. Step 3: launch the harness in the connector
directory and confirm the receipt line appears, with `status:verified` and the
expected tier. If the receipt is missing or stale, run the sync before doing any
work.

## Phase 6 — Save the handoff

Write the handoff document to the canon and report its id or path, the script's
path, and the list of repositories the owner still needs to run it against.

## Key principles

- Survey is read-only; the only writes are the handoff document and the script.
- Stashes are called out explicitly, every time.
- The receiving machine is verified by the receipt line, not by assumption.
